  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n0 0 8 0 8\n2 2 8 0 0\n2 2 0 0 8\n0 0 0 2 2\n8 8 0 2 2\nOutput:\n0\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n0 8 0 0 0 0 0\n2 2 0 8 8 8 0\n2 2 8 8 0 2 2\n0 0 8 0 0 2 2\n0 8 0 0 8 0 0\nOutput:\n8\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n8 2 2 8 8 0 0\n0 2 2 0 0 0 8\n0 8 8 0 0 8 0\n0 0 8 0 0 0 8\n8 0 8 8 8 2 2\n8 0 0 0 0 2 2\nOutput:\n8\n\nExample 4:\n\nInput:\n8 8 0 0 2 2 0\n0 8 8 0 2 2 8\n0 0 0 8 0 8 0\n8 0 0 0 0 0 0\n0 2 2 0 8 0 8\n0 2 2 8 8 0 8\nOutput:\n0\n\nExample 5:\n\nInput:\n8 0 0 0 0 8 0\n0 0 2 2 0 8 0\n8 0 2 2 0 0 0\n0 0 8 0 0 8 0\n0 0 8 2 2 0 8\n8 0 0 2 2 8 0\nOutput:\n8\n\nExample 6:\n\nInput:\n8 0 0 2 2 8\n8 0 8 2 2 0\n0 0 0 0 8 0\n2 2 8 0 8 0\n2 2 0 0 0 8\n0 8 8 0 8 0\nOutput:\n0\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n2 2 8 8 0 8\n2 2 0 8 0 0\n8 8 0 0 0 8\n0 8 8 8 0 0\n8 0 8 0 0 8\n0 0 8 2 2 0\n8 0 0 2 2 0\n0 8 0 0 0 8\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    # Find 2x2 blocks of 2s, check what value (0 or 8) is more common around/near them\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    blocks = []\n    for r in range(rows-1):\n        for c in range(cols-1):\n            if grid[r][c]==2 and grid[r][c+1]==2 and grid[r+1][c]==2 and grid[r+1][c+1]==2:\n                blocks.append((r,c))\n    \n    # Count 8s adjacent to each block\n    for br, bc in blocks:\n        pass\n    \n    # Maybe: for each 2x2 block, count 8s in neighboring cells, return 8 if majority, else 0\n    # Let me think differently - maybe it's about what value the 2x2 blocks are \"closer\" to or surrounded by\n    \n    # Count 8s adjacent (orthogonally) to the 2x2 block positions\n    total_8 = 0\n    total_0 = 0\n    for br, bc in blocks:\n        neighbors = set()\n        for r in [br, br+1]:\n            for c in [bc, bc+1]:\n                for dr, dc in [(-1,0),(1,0),(0,-1),(0,1)]:\n                    nr, nc = r+dr, c+dc\n                    if 0<=nr<rows and 0<=nc<cols and (nr,nc) not in [(br,bc),(br,bc+1),(br+1,bc),(br+1,bc+1)]:\n                        neighbors.add((nr,nc))\n        for nr,nc in neighbors:\n            if grid[nr][nc]==8: total_8+=1\n            elif grid[nr][nc]==0: total_0+=1\n    \n    if total_8 > total_0:\n        return [[8]]\n    else:\n        return [[0]]",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    # Find 2x2 blocks of 2s, check what value (0 or 8) is more common around/near them\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    blocks = []\n    for r in range(rows-1):\n        for c in range(cols-1):\n            if grid[r][c]==2 and grid[r][c+1]==2 and grid[r+1][c]==2 and grid[r+1][c+1]==2:\n                blocks.append((r,c))\n    \n    # Count 8s adjacent to each block\n    for br, bc in blocks:\n        pass\n    \n    # Maybe: for each 2x2 block, count 8s in neighboring cells, return 8 if majority, else 0\n    # Let me think differently - maybe it's about what value the 2x2 blocks are \"closer\" to or surrounded by\n    \n    # Count 8s adjacent (orthogonally) to the 2x2 block positions\n    total_8 = 0\n    total_0 = 0\n    for br, bc in blocks:\n        neighbors = set()\n        for r in [br, br+1]:\n            for c in [bc, bc+1]:\n                for dr, dc in [(-1,0),(1,0),(0,-1),(0,1)]:\n                    nr, nc = r+dr, c+dc\n                    if 0<=nr<rows and 0<=nc<cols and (nr,nc) not in [(br,bc),(br,bc+1),(br+1,bc),(br+1,bc+1)]:\n                        neighbors.add((nr,nc))\n        for nr,nc in neighbors:\n            if grid[nr][nc]==8: total_8+=1\n            elif grid[nr][nc]==0: total_0+=1\n    \n    if total_8 > total_0:\n        return [[8]]\n    else:\n        return [[0]]",
